hello welcome to rayleigh windmill  we're going to talk about some items  today that we've got on the museum floor  which is on the first floor of the  windmill the first item we're going to  talk about is the coal saver  and this was a small metal object which  you should be able to see a picture of  now  this object was placed into a fireplace  and the coal was put around it  when the fire was lit the coal saver  used to heat up and let out quite a bit  of heat therefore saving the use of coal  next item we're going to look at is a  hot water bottle  these are made of earthenware or  stoneware this particular one which you  can see a picture of now  would have been placed into your bed on  a cold day to warm up the sheets  and they were really nice to put your  feet on to keep them nice and warm  and the third item we're looking at is a  toaster thought to date back to the  1940s or 50s  you should see a picture now with the  open and closed the downside of this  toaster was that you had to put the  toast in  open it and then turn it round to cook  the other side  when we reopen the windmill come and  visit us and come up to the first floor  and visit the museum  there's lots of interesting items  concerning local history from rayleigh  including items from the first world war  the second world war  and lots more see you soon  you
 

